n><graphic xlink:href="ebo-04-181-g01"/></fig><fig id="f2-ebo-4-181" position="float"><label>Figure 2</label><caption><p>Distribution of sequence length of 106 genes.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="ebo-04-181-g02"/></fig><fig id="f3-ebo-4-181" position="float"><label>Figure 3</label><caption><p>The correlation between symmetric differences from trees to the MSG tree and gene lengths.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="ebo-04-181-g03"/></fig><fig id="f4-ebo-4-181" position="float"><label>Figure 4</label><caption><p>Relationship between gene-support percentage of unique trees and symmetric distances of the trees from the maximum gene-support tree. The symmetric difference is the number of steps required to convert between two trees. MP trees inferred from nucleotides were used here (no data shown for other methods included).</p><p>*Indicates statistical significance at the p = 0.05 level. Top panel: Full dataset; Bottom panel: After taking off the last point.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="ebo-04-181-g04"/></fig><fig id="f5-ebo-4-181" position="float"><label>Figure 5</label><caption><p>The rooted maximum gene-support tree based on 36 genes from seven plant species. <italic>G. biloba</italic> was specified as the outgroup.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="ebo-04-181-g05"/></fig><fig id="f6-ebo-4-181" position="float"><label>Figure 6</label><caption><p>Phylogenetic analyses of the concatenated alignments of 106 genes from seven yeast species. Numbers above branches are bootstrap values (ME on amino acids/NJ on amino acids/NJ on nucleotides).</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="ebo-04-181-g06"/></fig><table-wrap id="t1-ebo-4-181" position="float"><label>Table 1</label><caption><p>Maximum gene-support (MGS), weighted maximum gene-support (WMGS), the second highest gene-support (2nd HGS), weighted second highest gene-support (2nd WHGS), number of unique trees (NUT), and threshold gene number (TGN) required to overcome incongruence based on a data set of 106 genes from 